### 3.3 Damaged Fob

**What it does:** A member reports a fob as damaged. Differs from lost in that the system asks a diagnostic question (does the reader beep?) to help the approver decide if the member should pay for the replacement.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A{Authenticated?}
    A -->|Yes| B[Member: Select 'Report damaged fob']
    A -->|No| C[Guest: Prompted to sign in first]
    C --> D{Continue without signing in?}
    D -->|Sign in| E[Redirect to sign-in]
    D -->|Continue| F[Look up email and verify]

    B --> G[Member: Select specific fob]
    F --> G

    G --> H{Does the reader beep when swiped?}
    H -->|Yes - beeps but won't unlock| I[Likely user damage]
    H -->|No - nothing happens| J[Likely faulty fob]

    I --> K{Want a replacement?}
    J --> K

    K -->|Yes| L[System: Auto-request exemption]
    L --> M[AWAITING_EXEMPTION_REVIEW]
    M --> N{Approver: Review}
    N -->|Faulty fob - approve| O[AWAITING_APPROVAL - no payment]
    N -->|User damage - reject| P[AWAITING_PAYMENT - link emailed]
    P --> Q[Member: Pay]
    Q --> O
    O --> R[Normal approval pipeline]

    K -->|No| S[Deactivation only]
    S --> T[Credential: 'Reported Damaged']
    T --> U[Programmer confirms]
    U --> V[Credential: 'Inactive - Damaged']

    style V fill:#6b7280,color:#fff
```

**Key details:**
- Reader beep answer is recorded in audit and exemption reason
- Damaged fob replacements automatically request payment exemption - the approver decides if the member caused damage (pay) or it's a faulty fob (no pay)
- Same two-stage credential transition as lost fobs: `damaged` → `inactive_damaged`

