### 3.7 Access Update

**What it does:** A member's access level changes. Two triggers: member-initiated (e.g. "I've been appointed an officer") or system-initiated (officer term expired).

#### Member-Initiated

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Member: Select 'Request access update']
    A --> B[Member: Verify identity]
    B --> C[Member: Select reason for change]
    C --> D[Member: Review and submit]
    D --> E[AWAITING_APPROVAL]
    E --> F[Approver: Review with access level selector]
    F -->|Approve| G[Update membership access level + flags]
    G --> H[AWAITING_PROGRAMMING]
    H --> I[Programmer: Update member in Integriti]
    I --> J[ISSUED - no credential changes]
    F -->|Reject| K[REJECTED - can be reopened]

    style J fill:#22c55e,color:#fff
    style K fill:#ef4444,color:#fff
```

#### System-Initiated (see [Section 8.2](#82-officerboard-permission-expiry))

**Key details:**
- **Access level lives on the membership, not the credential.** When programming an access update, the programmer updates the member's access group in Integriti. The physical fob is not replaced.
- Always payment-exempt
- **Not for expired fobs.** The member form shows an amber notice (`role="status"`) as soon as "Request an access update" is selected (before OTP) and again on the reason step, personalised with the fob serial(s) (capped at three) when the verified member has an `expired` credential; the personalised variant repeats once more on the Review step. It tells them expired fobs are reactivated by the membership renewal (attestation) flow, that no access update is needed, and that an extra form slows the process. Advisory only; submission is not blocked. No deep link to renewal: attestation links are tokenised and sent by admins, so there is no member-facing renewal URL.
- ISSUE step does NOT create or deactivate credentials for access_update requests
- The request pipeline tracks the workflow; the actual data change is on `tenant_memberships.access_level_id`

